  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1237 AM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nWednesday through Monday:\n\nHigh pressure will pull to the east of Wisconsin Friday which\nshould swing winds back around to the east to southeast. This\nwill bring back some WAA to the state. There is a bit of\nuncertainty on if the the mid level WAA will be enough to get\nsome rain to develop. The forcing doesn't look all that great\nand moisture will still be lacking under the easterly winds.\nConditions for rain starts to improve a bit heading to Friday\nnight into Saturday as the trough advects northeast into the\nCentral Plains and western Great Lakes. This track is still\nuncertain however. The GFS looks to be the furtherest north,\nEURO the furthest south with the CAN and NAM being some where in\nbetween. The NAM by far seems to be the slowest with this system\nat the edge of its forecast range. All these difference shift\naround the axis of rain and the strength of the system as a\nwhole. At least we can bank on some consistency from the GFS as\nit remains the rainiest option for southern Wisconsin. Kept to\nthe average among guidance which keeps POPS low around 15-30%. \n\nLooking further ahead to into early next week, guidance is\nhinting at a larger and stronger low pressure system moving into\nthe central CONUS. There is a bit of discrepancies as one would\nexpect with the biggest caveats being how well the upper level\nflow merges together with a northern and southern track trough.\nThe evolution of this system differs wildly from model to \nmodel, but this looks to kick off a more active period \nregardless. \n\nPatterson",
